{
  "message": "I've drawn the setup you described as an AWS test design: a file lands in a private S3 bucket, an SQS queue picks it up, a Lambda counts the words, and DynamoDB saves one result per file version, so a duplicate delivery can't overwrite a result. Failed messages are retried, and after repeated failures they move to a dead-letter queue; Sydney (ap-southeast-2) is shown only as a candidate region, not an approved one, and nothing gets deployed. The next missing decision is about alerts: who should be told when a message lands in the dead-letter queue, and is there an existing AWS SNS alert topic we can use? Production needs one.",
  "phase": "Architecture",
  "proposal": {
    "baseRevision": 1,
    "title": "Async S3 → SQS → Lambda word-count pipeline (synthetic test design)",
    "body": "Scope: synthetic UTF-8 text files only, word counts saved per exact object version. Assumptions: AWS; Sydney (ap-southeast-2) is a candidate region, not an approved one; default limits are 256 MiB, 30 s timeout, concurrency 2, 1 MB input and 30-day logs. The SQS visibility timeout must be at least six times the function timeout. The generated resources include the execution role, logs, version, alias, event mapping and DLQ alarm. Production needs an existing, reviewed SNS alarm topic. This is a design, not a deployment.",
    "next": {
